`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"The escalation path and what ends it",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Start with the library implementation where one exists: it is already tuned and covers many shapes and dtypes. Profile the eager program to find a candidate, a chain of launches whose intermediates travel through HBM. Then try ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"}),", whose default backend is TorchInductor. For matrix multiplications (GEMMs) it can choose among specialized backends, one of them written in the CuTe DSL ",e.jsx(t,{id:"patel2026cutedslgemm",children:"Patel et al., 2026"}),". Slide 27 shows it fusing the five-kernel eager softmax into one kernel. Then profile the compiled program again. Write a custom kernel only for a boundary that still shows up, and only when the time it saves justifies maintaining the kernel."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A custom kernel starts from a hypothesis: which boundary it removes and how many bytes that saves. It ends with two pieces of evidence. The first is a correctness check against a trusted reference, run before any timing. The second is a benchmark that gives the kernel and its comparators the same shapes, dtypes, outputs, synchronization and measurement protocol. A faster time on a different shape or dtype, or with a looser output check, does not count as an improvement."})]})]})]}),`
