`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Inside one SM",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"This is Week 4's figure of the H100, zoomed into one streaming multiprocessor (SM). Its GPU button returns to the whole chip."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A few terms first. A thread runs the kernel's code. A warp is a group of 32 threads that execute together. Each thread is one lane of its warp. A cooperative thread array (CTA) is a group of up to 1,024 threads that run on one SM and share memory."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Each SM is split into four sub-partitions. Each sub-partition has a warp scheduler, a register file and its own execution units: integer and floating-point cores, load/store units, an SFU and a Tensor Core. The four sub-partitions share the SM's L1 data cache, shared memory and texture units ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"Units and registers of one H100 sub-partition",children:e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["On the H100 a sub-partition has 32 FP32 cores, 16 INT32 cores, 16 FP64 cores and one Tensor Core. These are a quarter of the SM's 128, 64, 64 and 4 ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),". Its register file holds 16,384 32-bit registers, 64 KB ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),"."]})}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["A warp is assigned to one sub-partition and stays there from launch to completion ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". Its threads' registers live in that sub-partition's register file. An SM holds at most 64 warps at a time, so, by our own count, each scheduler has at most 16 to choose from. On every cycle, each scheduler picks one warp that is ready to issue and issues its next instruction or instructions ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". A warp that waits for data from memory is not ready, so the scheduler issues from another warp in the meantime."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The softmax kernel's loads and stores go through the load/store units. The maximum, the subtraction, the sum and the scaling run on the floating-point cores. The exponential runs on the SFU. The kernel has no matrix product, so it never uses a Tensor Core."})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Data lives in four memory tiers",accent:2,number:"8",children:[e.jsx(x,{}),e.jsxs(s.table,{children:[e.jsx(s.thead,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Tier"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"H100 SXM size"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Shared by"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Rough latency"})]})}),e.jsxs(s.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Registers"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"256 KB per SM (64K × 32 bit)"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"one thread"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"lowest"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Shared memory"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"up to 228 KB per SM"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"one CTA"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 10–20 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"L2 cache"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"50 MB"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"all SMs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 150–200 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"HBM"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"80 GB at 3.35 TB/s"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"all SMs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 400 ns"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
