`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Sectors, lines and coalescing",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Global memory moves in aligned 32-byte sectors, and an L1 or L2 cache line holds four of them ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". When a warp loads, the hardware fetches every sector that any of its 32 lanes touches. If 32 lanes read 32 consecutive 4-byte words, the warp needs four sectors and uses every fetched byte ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026cudaguide",children:"NVIDIA, 2026a"}),". Such an access is coalesced. If lanes read scattered addresses, the warp can need one sector per lane. Each 32-byte sector then delivers only the 2 or 4 bytes its lane uses. Coalescing is a separate property from occupancy ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2024bestpractices",children:"NVIDIA, 2024a"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The width of each lane's load is a second property. On an H100, one load instruction moves at most 16 bytes per thread, eight bf16 values. Loads of 32 bytes need compute capability 10.0 or higher ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026ptx",children:"NVIDIA, 2026c"}),". A wider load fetches the same bytes with fewer instructions, and each instruction puts more bytes in flight. Slide 17 shows how the kernel's layout sets both properties."]})]})]})]}),`
