`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Tiles, matrix units and a pipeline",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Fast matrix kernels on an H100 do not work like the chapter's 2×2 example, where each thread computes single products on a CUDA core. A Tensor Core, one of the matrix units of Week 4, multiplies small matrix tiles, and the instruction that drives it belongs to a group of threads. With ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"mma"})," (matrix multiply-accumulate), one warp multiplies, for bf16, a 16 × 16 tile by a 16 × 8 tile. That is 2,048 multiply-adds in one instruction, with the operands spread over the registers of its 32 threads ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026ptx",children:"NVIDIA, 2026c"}),". Hopper's ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"wgmma"})," is issued by a warpgroup, four contiguous warps whose first warp's rank is a multiple of 4 ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026ptx",children:"NVIDIA, 2026c"})," ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),". It produces output tiles of 64 rows and up to 256 columns ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2026ptx",children:"NVIDIA, 2026c"}),". No thread multiplies a single pair of entries."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A production matrix multiplication kernel also gives each block a large tile of the output and loops over the shared dimension in chunks staged through shared memory. The upper levels stay as in the example: each block owns a part of the output on one SM. Only the bottom level changes, from single products on CUDA cores to tile products on Tensor Cores."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["FlashAttention splits Q, K and V into tiles that fit in shared memory. It computes ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"QKᵀ"})," and the product of the probabilities with ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"V"})," on Tensor Cores, one in each sub-partition. Hopper's Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA), the unit below the sub-partitions on slide 7, copies large blocks between global and shared memory asynchronously ",e.jsx(t,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),". So the next K/V tile arrives while the current one is in use, and data movement overlaps with the arithmetic. With warp specialization, producer warps only issue copies, consumer warps only compute, and shared-memory buffers pass the tiles between them. The schedule and the data movement change, and the result is still exact attention."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{optional:!0,title:"FlashAttention-4 rebalances the work for Blackwell",accent:6,number:"33",children:[e.jsxs(s.table,{children:[e.jsx(s.thead,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Per SM, per clock"}),e.jsx(s.th,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"Hopper"}),e.jsx(s.th,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"Blackwell B200"})]})}),e.jsxs(s.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"bf16 MMA operations"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"4,096"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"8,192"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Exponentials"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"16"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"16"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Shared memory bytes"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"128"}),e.jsx(s.td,{style:{textAlign:"right"},children:"128"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(s.li,{children:["Matrix units doubled, special-function unit unchanged ",e.jsx(t,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"10–25% of exponentials on fused multiply-add units"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Rescale only for max increases above 8"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Two softmax changes for a GPU whose matrix units outgrew the rest",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["FlashAttention-4 is a kernel designed together with its algorithm for one hardware imbalance ",e.jsx(t,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),". From Hopper to Blackwell, Tensor Core throughput doubled while other units grew more slowly or not at all ",e.jsx(t,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),". Per SM and clock, the B200 performs 8,192 bf16 MMA operations against Hopper's 4,096. Its special-function unit still computes 16 exponentials, and its shared memory still delivers 128 bytes, as on Hopper. The matrix products of attention got faster and the exponentials did not, so the exponentials take a larger share of the time."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["FlashAttention-4 changes the softmax in two ways. First, it computes ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"2^x"})," in software for 10 to 25% of the entries in each row. It does so on the fused multiply-add (FMA) units, the FP32 units that compute ",e.jsx(i,{math:"a \\cdot b + c",display:!1})," in one instruction. A range reduction and a polynomial of degree 3 match the hardware result to within one bf16 unit in the last place. The FMA units and the special-function unit then work in parallel."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Second, it rescales only when the maximum grows by more than a threshold ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"τ"}),", typically ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"log₂ 256 = 8"}),". The threshold is in base-2 units, because FlashAttention-4, like our kernel, evaluates the exponential as ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"2^x"}),". A stale maximum lets exponentials reach at most 256, which fp32 holds easily. Because ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o"})," use the same stale maximum, it cancels in ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o / l"}),". When the lanes of one warp take different branches, the warp runs both paths one after the other. To avoid this divergence, all lanes of a warp rescale together when any one needs it ",e.jsx(t,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{optional:!0,title:"Read production kernels by their roles",accent:5,number:"34",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
