`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"A guide for optional source reading",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"To go further, label each block of a production kernel with one of four roles: layout (which thread owns which data), partition (how the tensor is cut into tiles and slices), reduction or MMA (the arithmetic) and pipeline (how data moves between tiers and overlaps with compute). Recognizing the roles is enough."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["QuACK's softmax reaches the copy's speed on UC3's H100 with a mapping different from the course kernel's ",e.jsx(t,{id:"daoailab2026quack",children:"Dao-AILab, 2026"}),". ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"_threads_per_row"})," picks 64 threads for a 4,096-wide row. CTAs have 128 threads for rows up to 16K wide, so one CTA handles two rows. The vector width is 128 bits divided by the element width, eight bf16 values, so each thread loads 16 bytes at a time. The data goes into shared memory through asynchronous copies (",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"cp.async"}),") and then into registers. The maximum and the sum are computed together, in the online form of slide 29, so one exchange through shared memory replaces the course kernel's two. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"row_reduce"})," in ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"reduce.py"})," combines the thread, warp and CTA tiers, as the course kernel's helper does. Very wide rows are split across a cluster of CTAs. For 16-bit rows that starts above 16K elements, so at 4,096 the cluster has one CTA ",e.jsx(t,{id:"daoailab2026quack",children:"Dao-AILab, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["In FlashAttention-4, find the tile layouts, the MMA instructions, the softmax with its conditional rescaling, and the producer and consumer warps ",e.jsx(t,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
