`]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"The answer in two factors",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The measured 4.9 is the product of two factors, both derived on slide 4. The eager chain sends every intermediate tensor to HBM and back, so it moves four times the bytes of a fused kernel. It also moves them at a lower share of a plain copy's bandwidth. Softmax lies far left of every ridge, so its time follows the bytes it moves."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A fused kernel removes the intermediate passes because its data stays on the chip. In the course kernel one CTA handles one row. It keeps the row in fp32 registers through both sweeps, first for the maximum, then for the exponentials and their sum. It uses shared memory only for the 32 warp partials."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Fusion alone does not reach the copy's speed. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"})," and the walkthrough kernel ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"})," are single kernels too. Yet they reach only 53.9% and 68.1% of the copy. HBM delivers its bandwidth only when enough bytes are in flight, averaged over time, to cover its latency (slide 13). At the same occupancy, ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"})," can put twice the bytes in flight with its 16-byte loads and reaches 99.3% of the copy, though the run cannot separate the wider loads from its halved warp count. QuACK reaches 98.9% (slide 27)."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Fusion does not require writing a kernel by hand. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"})," turned the five eager kernels into one, 3.5 times faster than the eager chain. A custom kernel is worth its upkeep only for a boundary the compiler leaves in place, where separate kernels still pass an intermediate through HBM (slide 6)."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Reflection questions",accent:5,number:"36",items:"4",long:"true",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"No submission. Bring short answers to the Week 6 tutorial."}),e.jsxs(s.ol,{children:[`
